The `contains`, `start with` and `end with` branches all pass `escape="\\"`. The `not contains` branch escapes the value but doesn't declare an escape character:

::diff{file="api/core/rag/retrieval/dataset_retrieval.py" lines="1200-1214"}

On PostgreSQL and MySQL this happens to work, because both default to backslash as the LIKE escape. It still depends on dialect defaults. It breaks under MySQL `NO_BACKSLASH_ESCAPES`, and on any backend without that default (for example SQLite in tests). It is also inconsistent with the sibling branches. An earlier revision of this branch had `escape="\\"` here, so this looks like an accidental drop.

::suggestion{file="api/core/rag/retrieval/dataset_retrieval.py" lines="1207-1207"}
                filters.append(json_field.notlike(f"%{escaped_value}%", escape="\\"))

The PR comments out the module-level `from services.app_service import AppService` in two test files. It then adds about 20 copies of an in-function import, each with the comment "avoid circular dependency":

::diff{file="api/tests/test_containers_integration_tests/services/test_app_service.py" lines="10-12"}

None of the production changes add a new import edge. `app_service.py` imports `libs.helper` lazily inside a function, and `libs.helper` doesn't import any services. So it isn't clear what cycle this works around. If a cycle really exists, it should be fixed in production code. If not, this is churn that makes the diff harder to review and leaves commented-out code behind.

